# Copy to .env.local and fill in. .env.local is gitignored; this file is not, and
# must never gain values. Secrets in deployed environments come from SSM Parameter
# Store SecureStrings at /guestnote/<env>/*, never from a committed file.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- database ----
# Neon, aws-eu-central-1. Connect as `app_user` -- NOT the table owner, and without
# BYPASSRLS, or every RLS policy in packages/db/migrations silently does nothing.
#
# POOLED is what the app uses (the -pooler host). withTenant() needs interactive
# transactions, so it runs over the WebSocket/pooled driver.
DATABASE_URL=

# UNPOOLED is for drizzle-kit migrate only. DDL through a transaction-mode pooler
# is a bad time; run migrations against the direct endpoint.
DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED=

# The Neon branch the `db` vitest project runs against. Point this at a throwaway
# branch, not at `main` -- the isolation suite writes fixtures and truncates.
# It must be the POOLED host: assertions 3 and 4 in packages/db/test/pooling.test.ts
# are about pooler behaviour and are meaningless against a direct connection.
TEST_DATABASE_URL=

# The privileged role the `db` suite seeds fixtures with, on the same branch. It must
# be a DIFFERENT role from TEST_DATABASE_URL and must be able to bypass RLS, or the
# fixtures cannot be written and the suite reports "47 passed / 48 skipped, zero
# failures" -- which looks like success. assertSeedRoleUsable() throws before the
# first INSERT rather than letting that happen.
SEED_DATABASE_URL=

# --------------------------------------------------------------------- web ----
# The root domain proxy.ts resolves hosts against, with the port stripped. Locally
# this is `localhost`, so `app.localhost:3000` and `els-en-jan.localhost:3000` take
# the same branches the deployed app does -- Chrome, Edge and Firefox resolve any
# *.localhost to loopback with no DNS (RFC 6761). Safari on macOS does not; see
# apps/web/README.md for the /etc/hosts fallback.
GUESTNOTE_ROOT_DOMAIN=localhost

# The single label the dashboard answers on. `pro` 301-redirects to it. Everything
# else under the root domain is a tenant slug or a reserved word.
GUESTNOTE_APP_SUBDOMAIN=app

# -------------------------------------------------------------------- auth ----
# openssl rand -base64 32
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- mail ----
# Which transport to build: `ses` sends for real, `console` renders to
# apps/web/.mail/*.html and prints the code.
#
# LEAVE THIS UNSET. Omitted, it resolves to `console` in development and `ses`
# everywhere else -- which is the right way round, because the value you get by
# forgetting is the safe one. Setting it to `console` in a deployed environment
# would write sign-in codes into CloudWatch and never send them, and
# apps/web/src/lib/mailer.ts throws rather than let that happen.
#
# Set it to `ses` locally when you specifically want a real email in a real inbox
# -- checking a template renders in Gmail, or testing sign-in end to end. Two
# things bite, in this order:
#
#   1. Credentials. See the note at the bottom of this file.
#   2. The account is STILL IN SANDBOX, deliberately (ADR 0002). SES will only
#      accept a destination that is a verified identity or under the verified
#      `guestnote.be` domain; anything else comes back MessageRejected, which
#      packages/email/src/ses.ts maps to `rejected` and the sign-in screen shows
#      as "we could not deliver". That is not a bug in the mailer.
#
#      Check who is allowed before blaming the code:
#        aws sesv2 list-email-identities --profile guestnote
#        aws sesv2 get-account --profile guestnote   # ProductionAccessEnabled
#
# Unset it again afterwards. Sandbox is 200/day at 1/sec, shared with everything
# else, and the console transport is faster to read than an inbox anyway.
GUESTNOTE_MAIL_TRANSPORT=

# The region SES, the verified identity and the `guestnote-default` configuration
# set all live in. Lambda injects this; .envrc exports it locally; the default in
# env.ts covers everything else. A wrong value fails confusingly -- an
# unverified-identity error against an account that HAS verified the identity, in
# another region.
AWS_REGION=eu-central-1

# NOT here, on purpose: the From address, the From name and the configuration set
# name. They are identical in every environment, so they are constants in
# apps/web/src/lib/mailer.ts rather than four more parameters to forget in SSM.
# The From address is also a deliverability decision, not a config one: ADR 0002
# set a custom MAIL FROM of mail.guestnote.be so SPF and DKIM both align on the
# domain, and sending as anything else silently loses that.

# NOT here either, and this is the one that will bite: sending needs AWS
# credentials. Locally that is the `guestnote` SSO profile from .envrc, so run
# `aws sso login --sso-session flowsha` before setting the transport to `ses`.
# In deployed environments it is the Lambda execution role, which M1a creates
# with ses:SendEmail scoped to the identity ARN.
